{"content":"The regression was in sqlparse/utils.py's imt() helper: it now dereferences token.ttype without first checking for None, whereas many call sites in grouping.py pass tokens that can legitimately be None (e.g. results of token_next/token_prev at the boundaries of a token list, or an absent nidx). This causes `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'ttype'` for statements where such boundary conditions arise (invalid/incomplete identifiers, trailing ORDER BY clauses, `AS` at the end of a statement, etc.) - matching the reported symptoms ('grouping identifier invalid', 'issue284 as grouping', 8 'parse order' cases, and one more).

Fix applied: added an early `if token is None: return False` guard at the top of `imt()` in sqlparse/utils.py, restoring the previous safe behavior (None token -> no match) instead of raising.

Verified with a few manual sanity checks (basic SELECT, ORDER BY with identifier list, assignment `:=`, `AS` aliasing) — all parse without error and produce sensible groupings. No tests directory was present in this checkout to run the hidden test suite directly, but the fix directly addresses the reported AttributeError and is a minimal, targeted change that shouldn't affect any currently-passing behavior (previously `imt` would only be called with non-None tokens in the paths that worked, so the new guard is purely additive safety).","tool_calls":[{"id":"cli_9_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "import sqlparse; import sqlparse.sql as sql; print(sqlparse.parse('SELECT foo AS bar FROM mytable')[0]); print(sqlparse.parse('foo(int)')[0]._pprint_tree() if False else 'ok'); print(sqlparse.parse('SELECT a FROM foo ORDER BY a NULLS FIRST')[0]); print(sqlparse.parse('SELECT a FROM foo ORDER BY 1')[0]); print(sqlparse.parse('SELECT a FROM foo ORDER BY a DESC, b ASC')[0]); print(sqlparse.parse('foo.bar')[0])""}}]}

{"content":"The regression was in `sqlparse/utils.py`'s `imt()` helper function, which is used throughout `sqlparse/engine/grouping.py` as a `valid`/`match` helper. It dereferenced `token.ttype` without first checking whether `token` was `None`. Many grouping callbacks pass in tokens obtained from `token_next`/`token_prev` (e.g. `prev_`, `next_`) which can legitimately be `None` at the boundaries of a token list (end of statement, missing next/prev token, etc.). This caused `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'ttype'` for various inputs, matching the reported symptoms across 'grouping identifier invalid', 'issue284 as grouping', 8 'parse order' cases, and one more.

Fix: added an early `if token is None: return False` guard at the top of `imt()` in `sqlparse/utils.py`, restoring prior safe behavior (None token → no match) instead of raising.

Verified manually via `python -c` sanity checks covering: basic SELECT, ORDER BY with identifier lists/NULLS FIRST/ASC/DESC, assignment (`:=`), AS aliasing, and period grouping (`foo.bar`) — all parse successfully without error and produce sensible token groupings.","tool_calls":[{"id":"final_1","name":"finish","arguments":{"summary":"Fixed AttributeError regression: added `if token is None: return False` guard at the top of imt() in sqlparse/utils.py. This helper is called from many grouping.py match/valid callbacks with tokens (e.g. from token_next/token_prev) that can legitimately be None at token-list boundaries; the previous unguarded code crashed with 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'ttype'. Verified via manual sanity parses (SELECT, ORDER BY variants, assignment :=, AS aliasing, period grouping) all succeed post-fix."}}]}
